insert into public.flashcards (id, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('f1-001','core',1,'1.1',$$What are the four CATEGORIES of security controls?$$,$$Technical (implemented by systems), Managerial (policies/oversight), Operational (performed by people), Physical (real-world barriers).$$),
('f1-002','core',1,'1.1',$$What are the six control TYPES?$$,$$Preventive, Deterrent, Detective, Corrective, Compensating, Directive.$$),
('f1-003','core',1,'1.1',$$A security guard is what category and what type(s) of control?$$,$$Physical category; primarily preventive and deterrent (can also be detective).$$),
('f1-004','core',1,'1.1',$$What is a compensating control? Give an example.$$,$$An alternative control used when the primary control is not feasible - e.g., network segmentation and monitoring for a legacy system that cannot be patched.$$),
('f1-005','core',1,'1.1',$$What is a directive control? Give an example.$$,$$A control that directs behavior toward secure practice - e.g., an acceptable use policy or a "authorized personnel only" sign.$$),
('f1-006','core',1,'1.1',$$CCTV cameras are what type(s) of control?$$,$$Detective (recording/review) and deterrent (visible presence). Physical category.$$),
('f1-007','core',1,'1.2',$$Define the CIA triad.$$,$$Confidentiality (only authorized access), Integrity (data unaltered and trustworthy), Availability (accessible when needed).$$),
('f1-008','core',1,'1.2',$$What is non-repudiation and what provides it?$$,$$Inability to deny having performed an action; provided by digital signatures (signed with the sender's private key).$$),
('f1-009','core',1,'1.2',$$In AAA, what is the difference between authentication and authorization?$$,$$Authentication proves WHO you are; authorization determines WHAT you may do. Accounting logs what you did.$$),
('f1-010','core',1,'1.2',$$What is the gap analysis in security?$$,$$Comparing current security posture against a desired standard/framework to identify missing controls.$$),
('f1-011','core',1,'1.2',$$Name the core principles of Zero Trust.$$,$$Never trust, always verify: authenticate/authorize every request; least privilege; assume breach; continuous verification regardless of network location.$$),
('f1-012','core',1,'1.2',$$In the Zero Trust model, what are the Control Plane and Data Plane?$$,$$Control plane decides (adaptive identity, policy engine/administrator deciding access); data plane enforces and carries traffic (policy enforcement points, implicit trust zones).$$),
('f1-013','core',1,'1.2',$$What is a honeypot? What is a honeynet?$$,$$Honeypot: decoy system to attract/observe attackers. Honeynet: a network of honeypots simulating an environment.$$),
('f1-014','core',1,'1.2',$$What are honeyfiles and honeytokens?$$,$$Honeyfile: decoy file (e.g., fake passwords.xlsx) that alerts when accessed. Honeytoken: fake credential/data planted so its use reveals compromise.$$),
('f1-015','core',1,'1.2',$$What is an air-gapped system?$$,$$A system physically isolated from other networks - no network connection to the outside; data moves only via controlled physical media.$$),
('f1-016','core',1,'1.2',$$Bollards protect against what?$$,$$Vehicle-based attacks/ramming - physical preventive control for building perimeters.$$),
('f1-017','core',1,'1.2',$$What is an access control vestibule (mantrap)?$$,$$Two interlocking doors where only one opens at a time - prevents tailgating/piggybacking into secure areas.$$),
('f1-018','core',1,'1.2',$$Compare infrared, pressure, microwave, and ultrasonic sensors.$$,$$Infrared: detects body heat. Pressure: detects weight on a surface. Microwave: detects movement via radio reflections (penetrates some walls). Ultrasonic: detects motion via sound reflections.$$),
('f1-019','core',1,'1.3',$$Why does change management matter to security?$$,$$Uncontrolled changes cause outages and unreviewed security impact; formal approval, testing, and documentation keep systems in a known-secure state.$$),
('f1-020','core',1,'1.3',$$In change management, what is a backout plan?$$,$$A documented procedure to revert a change if it fails - required before implementation.$$),
('f1-021','core',1,'1.3',$$What is a maintenance window?$$,$$A scheduled, low-impact time period during which approved changes are implemented.$$),
('f1-022','core',1,'1.3',$$What is an allow list vs a deny list?$$,$$Allow list: only explicitly approved items run/pass (default deny - stronger). Deny list: everything runs except listed items (default allow).$$),
('f1-023','core',1,'1.3',$$Technical implications of change: name three things a change might unexpectedly affect.$$,$$Dependencies between systems, legacy applications, service/application restarts, downtime windows - all must be assessed before approval.$$),
('f1-024','core',1,'1.4',$$Symmetric vs asymmetric encryption - key difference and typical use?$$,$$Symmetric: one shared key, fast, bulk data (AES). Asymmetric: public/private key pair, slow, key exchange and signatures (RSA/ECC). Hybrid systems use asymmetric to share a symmetric session key.$$),
('f1-025','core',1,'1.4',$$Encrypting with the recipient's PUBLIC key provides what? Signing with your PRIVATE key provides what?$$,$$Public-key encryption: confidentiality (only their private key decrypts). Private-key signing: authenticity, integrity, non-repudiation.$$),
('f1-026','core',1,'1.4',$$What is key exchange and which algorithm is classically used?$$,$$Securely establishing a shared secret over an insecure channel; Diffie-Hellman (or ECDH).$$),
('f1-027','core',1,'1.4',$$Order these by scope: file, volume, partition, disk encryption.$$,$$File (single files) < volume (a mounted volume) < partition (disk partition) < full-disk (everything incl. OS). Also: database, record-level, transport.$$),
('f1-028','core',1,'1.4',$$What is a hash function and its key properties?$$,$$One-way function producing a fixed-length digest: deterministic, fast, preimage-resistant (cannot reverse), collision-resistant; small input change flips the output (avalanche).$$),
('f1-029','core',1,'1.4',$$What is salting and what attack does it defeat?$$,$$Adding a unique random value to each password before hashing; defeats rainbow-table (precomputed hash) attacks and hides duplicate passwords.$$),
('f1-030','core',1,'1.4',$$What is key stretching? Name two algorithms.$$,$$Deliberately slowing hashing with many iterations to make brute force expensive - bcrypt, PBKDF2 (also scrypt, Argon2).$$),
('f1-031','core',1,'1.4',$$Digital signature: what exactly is signed and how is it verified?$$,$$A hash of the message is encrypted with the sender's private key; the receiver decrypts with the sender's public key and compares to their own hash of the message.$$),
('f1-032','core',1,'1.4',$$What does a TPM provide?$$,$$Hardware root of trust on a device: secure key generation/storage, platform integrity measurements (secure boot), disk-encryption key protection.$$),
('f1-033','core',1,'1.4',$$HSM vs TPM?$$,$$TPM: per-device chip for that platform's keys. HSM: dedicated (often network/rack or PCIe) appliance for an organization's high-volume key storage and crypto operations (e.g., CA keys).$$),
('f1-034','core',1,'1.4',$$What is a secure enclave?$$,$$An isolated hardware region of a processor that protects secrets/computation even from a compromised OS.$$),
('f1-035','core',1,'1.4',$$Steganography vs encryption vs obfuscation?$$,$$Encryption makes data unreadable without a key; steganography HIDES data inside other data (e.g., an image); obfuscation makes data/code hard to understand (masking, tokenization).$$),
('f1-036','core',1,'1.4',$$What is tokenization and where is it common?$$,$$Replacing sensitive data with a non-sensitive token mapped in a secure vault; common for payment card data - the token is useless if stolen.$$),
('f1-037','core',1,'1.4',$$What is data masking?$$,$$Obscuring part of a value for display (e.g., ****-****-****-1234) so full sensitive data is not exposed.$$),
('f1-038','core',1,'1.4',$$What is the blockchain (exam view) and its security property?$$,$$An open public ledger of transactions distributed across many nodes; integrity - past records are practically immutable because blocks are hash-chained and replicated.$$),
('f1-039','core',1,'1.4',$$Walk through the certificate lifecycle.$$,$$Generate key pair -> CSR to CA -> CA validates identity and signs certificate -> deploy -> renew before expiration -> revoke if compromised (CRL/OCSP).$$),
('f1-040','core',1,'1.4',$$What is a wildcard certificate?$$,$$One certificate covering a domain and all first-level subdomains (*.example.com) - convenient, but compromise affects every covered host.$$),
('f1-041','core',1,'1.4',$$What is a self-signed certificate and its limitation?$$,$$A certificate signed by its own creator, not a trusted CA - provides encryption but no third-party trust; browsers warn unless manually trusted.$$),
('f1-042','core',1,'1.4',$$What is certificate pinning?$$,$$An application hard-codes/expects a specific certificate or key, rejecting others - defeats fraudulent certificates from compromised CAs.$$),
('f1-043','core',1,'1.4',$$What is a root of trust / certificate chaining?$$,$$Trust flows from a root CA down through intermediate CAs to leaf certificates; validating a cert means validating the whole chain to a trusted root.$$),
('f1-044','core',1,'1.4',$$OCSP stapling - what problem does it solve?$$,$$The web server fetches a signed OCSP response and delivers ("staples") it during the TLS handshake - faster revocation checking and no client privacy leak to the CA.$$),
('f1-045','core',1,'1.4',$$Third-party certificate vs private CA - when use which?$$,$$Public/third-party CA for internet-facing services (externally trusted); private/internal CA for internal-only services where you control trust stores.$$);
